            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Spiritual freedom in Christ from bondage to sin, death, and the law, which enables believers to live righteously and serve God. Secondary dimensions include Jubilee liberation, freedom of conscience regarding indifferent matters, and freedom from the Mosaic law.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A Jubilee release from bondage to sin into God's household and faithful service, explicitly characterized as a change of masters that does not entail freedom from the law's obligations.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Spiritual liberation from sin, death, and the law for the purpose of righteous living.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Jubilee release from sin into faithful service, changing masters rather than removing lawful obligations.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus broadly includes freedom from the Mosaic law (its condemnation, guardianship, or ceremonial requirements) as a fundamental component of Christian liberty. The book strictly excludes 'freedom from the law's obligations' from the definition of liberty.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book explicitly denies that liberty includes freedom from the law's obligations, whereas the consensus identifies freedom from the law as a key aspect of New Testament liberty.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Both agree on the core meaning of liberty as spiritual release from sin into the service of God (not a license to sin). The book refines this by applying the Jubilee typology and explicitly narrowing the boundary to exclude freedom from the law's obligations.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The Apostle Paul repeatedly connects Christian liberty with being freed from the law (e.g., Romans 7:1-6, Galatians 5:1-4), arguing believers are no longer under the law's guardianship or ceremonial requirements. The book's blanket denial of 'freedom from the law's obligations' may obscure this major New Testament theme.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
